WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
SPEAKER 1: Hope
is not a strategy

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:03.030
when it comes to security.

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:05.370
You have to approach
security from all angles

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.710
without a lot of burden
on dev or psych ops.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:08.738
Not the easiest task.

00:00:08.738 --> 00:00:11.280
But there's something on Google
Cloud that can go a long way.

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.242
Stay tuned.

00:00:13.242 --> 00:00:18.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:20.500
With an ever increasing
number of end points,

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:22.790
networks, and attack
surfaces, setting

00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:24.970
automated and trickle-down
security policies

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:27.400
across your cloud infrastructure
can be a challenge

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:28.840
to say the least.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:30.400
On top of that,
administrators want

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.280
to set guardrails to ensure
that their workloads are always

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:36.070
compliant to their security
requirements and industry

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.360
regulations.

00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:39.400
Public IP are one
of the easiest ways

00:00:39.400 --> 00:00:42.190
to expose your enterprise
environment to the internet,

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:44.590
so limiting public
IPs is paramount

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:46.900
when it comes to securing
your environments.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:48.580
To do that, you
need to understand

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.370
what resources use public
IP is in your network,

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:56.170
and that can include VMs, load
balancers, and VPN gateways.

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.390
When you start to deploy
production level systems,

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:01.090
you're looking at thousands of
ways in which your developers

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.740
can open public IP addresses.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.850
This is where organization
policies come into play.

00:01:06.850 --> 00:01:09.520
Org policies give you
a centralized guardrail

00:01:09.520 --> 00:01:12.460
over your organization's
Google Cloud resources.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:14.440
As the Org policy
administrator, you

00:01:14.440 --> 00:01:17.110
can configure restrictions
across your entire resource

00:01:17.110 --> 00:01:18.610
hierarchy.

00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:19.930
It works like this.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.860
You can set Org policies on the
Org on folders or on projects.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.860
They can be inherited by
nested folders and projects,

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:30.000
or they can be overwritten
on a case-by-case basis.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:31.960
Using Org policies,
the Org admin

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.540
can enforce constraints
on Google Cloud resources

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.720
such as VMs and load balancers
to adhere to basic security

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:39.580
requirements at all times.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:40.990
In this video,
we'll show you how

00:01:40.990 --> 00:01:43.270
you can use Org
policies as guardrails

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:46.210
to ensure no public IPs are
allowed in your Google Cloud

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:46.990
network.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:49.240
It's a perfect tool for
IT or security admins

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.180
to ensure all cloud deployments
adhere to their security

00:01:52.180 --> 00:01:53.260
standards.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:55.810
Let's dive into how you can
limit public IP exposure

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:59.800
for VMs, load balancers, and
VPN gateways using Org policies.

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:02.470
Compute Engine instances can
be exposed to the internet

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:05.980
directly when you assign
the VM a public IP,

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:07.690
or when you use
protocol forwarding

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:09.610
with the VM as its end point.

00:02:09.610 --> 00:02:11.410
To prevent Compute
Engine instances

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.390
from getting public
IPs, first make

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:16.430
sure you have the Org policy
admin role on the organization

00:02:16.430 --> 00:02:19.000
so you can add and
edit Org policies.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.160
Then, under Org
policies, search for

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:24.850
and edit the Org policy
constraint constraints compute

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:27.010
VM external IP access.

00:02:27.010 --> 00:02:29.410
This constraint lets you define
the set of Compute Engine

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.560
VMs that are allowed to use
public IPs on your network.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:36.550
Meanwhile, all other VMs will
not be able to get a public IP.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:41.920
Click Edit, select Customize,
replace the parent's policy

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:46.150
and use these roles, leave
the policy value as custom,

00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:49.060
and set policy type as allow.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:51.200
Then paste the path
to the only instance

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.100
you want to allow
external IP creation for.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:57.490
Now you've restricted public IP
creation to only this instance

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:00.610
and prevented public IP
creation for any other instances

00:03:00.610 --> 00:03:02.320
in your organization.

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.900
To prevent protocol
forwarding from being enabled,

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:08.260
use the Org policy constraint
constraints compute restrict

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:10.690
protocol forwarding
creation for types,

00:03:10.690 --> 00:03:12.790
and set it to the
following values.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:15.560
Note that the policy
value is case sensitive.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:18.880
This constraint lets you limit
virtual hosting of public IPs

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.840
by Compute Engine VM instances
in your organization.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.320
For VPNs, a VPN gateway requires
a public IP address for you

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:30.030
to connect your on premises
environment to Google Cloud.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:32.470
To ensure that your VPN
gateway is protected,

00:03:32.470 --> 00:03:34.810
use the Org policy
constraint constraints

00:03:34.810 --> 00:03:37.480
compute restrict VPN peer IPs.

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:39.430
This constraint will
limit the public IPs

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:42.670
that are allowed to initiate
IPsec sessions with your VPN

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:44.170
gateway.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:45.910
Next up, load balancers.

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:48.790
Google Cloud offers a variety
of internal and external load

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:49.600
balancers.

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:52.370
To prevent the creation of all
external load balancer types,

00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:54.670
usually org policy
constraint constraints

00:03:54.670 --> 00:03:58.040
compute restrict load
balancer creation for types,

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:00.460
and then make sure to add
all of these external load

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.340
balancer types for
the policy values.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:06.310
And for ease of use, instead
of manually entering,

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:09.580
you can also simply
add in colon external,

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:11.770
which will always cover
all types of external load

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:12.700
balancers.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.190
As new load balancer
types or introduced,

00:04:15.190 --> 00:04:17.050
you can be assured your
infrastructure will

00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:18.940
remain secure.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:21.640
And finally, restricting
GKE surfaces.

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:24.580
GKE lets developers create
and expose their services

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.510
to the internet very easily.

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:29.290
But if you add the policies
for VMs and load balancers,

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:31.780
as we showed, no
new GKE services

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:34.120
can be exposed to the internet
without the organization

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:35.740
administrator's knowledge.

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:39.070
In this example, we've enabled
our Org policy constraints.

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:41.680
A developer attempts
to create a GKE service

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:43.750
with an external load balancer.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:45.970
When we check the
status of the service,

00:04:45.970 --> 00:04:48.760
they get a pending external IP.

00:04:48.760 --> 00:04:51.520
When running kubectl
describe service,

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.980
they get an error due to
the load balancer Org policy

00:04:53.980 --> 00:04:55.420
constraint in place.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.430
Pretty sweet.

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.160
Keep in mind Org policies
are not retroactive.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.740
They will only apply to
new infrastructure requests

00:05:02.740 --> 00:05:04.270
after the policy is set.

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:07.150
So you won't have to worry about
breaking any existing workloads

00:05:07.150 --> 00:05:09.540
when you add these
policies to your Org.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:10.300
And that's it.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.180
You can apply Org policies
easily and efficiently

00:05:13.180 --> 00:05:15.670
across your entire Org
hierarchy or subset

00:05:15.670 --> 00:05:18.248
of resources from a
single centralized place.

00:05:18.248 --> 00:05:19.790
You can sleep better
at night knowing

00:05:19.790 --> 00:05:22.460
you don't have any stray
resources that your teams have

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:25.520
assigned public IPs to
that shouldn't have them.

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:27.150
Check out the link
below to learn more.

00:05:27.150 --> 00:05:29.060
And remember,
optimizing your network

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:31.800
means freeing up your bandwidth.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:35.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

